{"step_index":111,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:57:49Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:57:49+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:57:49+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/lean_export.py`\nTotal Lines: 244\nTotal Bytes: 11198\nShowing lines 1 to 100\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: \"\"\"\n2: pipeline/lean_export.py — well-formed Lean 4 export for the *supported* subset of\n3: CEGIS survivors.\n4: \n5: graffiti3 will happily render any conjecture to a Lean stub, but a stub over\n6: undefined symbols (``domination_number G``) can never be kernel-verified. This\n7: module restricts export to conjectures whose invariants are backed by the\n8: ``LeanProject.GraphInvariants`` preamble (mathlib defs + a small domination\n9: layer), maps each invariant to its real Lean name, and emits a self-contained,\n10: compilable ``theorem … := sorry`` (imports + binders + ``open SimpleGraph``) that\n11: a prover can actually close.\n12: \n13: Out of scope (silently skipped, not mis-formalized): invariants with no faithful\n14: mathlib definition yet (zero forcing, residue, Roman domination, …) and\n15: conjectures conditioned on a graph class with no formalization (chordal, planar).\n16: \"\"\"\n17: from __future__ import annotations\n18: \n19: import re\n20: from typing import Dict, List, Optional\n21: \n22: from pipeline import linear_form\n23: from pipeline.lean_disproof import definedness_hyps\n24: \n25: # pipeline invariant column  →  Lean expression over `(G : SimpleGraph V)`.\n26: # Only invariants with a faithful definition in LeanProject.GraphInvariants (or\n27: # mathlib, re-exported there) appear here.\n28: SUPPORTED: Dict[str, str] = {\n29:     \"order\":                         \"G.order\",\n30:     \"size\":                          \"G.size\",\n31:     \"minimum_degree\":                \"G.minDegree\",\n32:     \"maximum_degree\":                \"G.maxDegree\",\n33:     \"cl\n<truncated 1777 bytes>\negular\": \"IsRegularClass\", \"cubic\": \"IsCubicClass\",\n65:     \"subcubic\": \"IsSubcubicClass\", \"triangle_free\": \"IsTriangleFreeClass\",\n66:     \"K_4_free\": \"IsK4FreeClass\", \"bipartite\": \"IsBipartiteClass\",\n67:     \"eulerian\": \"IsEulerianClass\",\n68:     # mathlib has these directly (`Connected`, `IsTree`)\n69:     \"connected\": \"IsConnectedClass\", \"tree\": \"IsTreeClass\",\n70:     # forbidden induced subgraph on four vertices: K₁,₃ and P₄ respectively\n71:     \"claw_free\": \"IsClawFreeClass\", \"cograph\": \"IsCographClass\",\n72:     # graphcalc's auto-base hypothesis on every Graffiti3 conjecture: |V| ≥ 2.\n73:     \"nontrivial\": \"IsNontrivialClass\",\n74: }\n75: _REL_LEAN = {\"≤\": \"≤\", \"≥\": \"≥\", \"<=\": \"≤\", \">=\": \"≥\", \"=\": \"=\"}\n76: \n77: \n78: def _condition_classes(cond: str, columns) -> List[str]:\n79:     return [c for c in columns if re.search(r\"\\b\" + re.escape(c) + r\"\\b\", cond)]\n80: \n81: \n82: def _bare_invariant(side: str, columns) -> Optional[str]:\n83:     \"\"\"A side that is exactly one supported invariant (no coefficient/offset/\n84:     product), else None.\"\"\"\n85:     invs = [c for c in columns if re.search(r\"\\b\" + re.escape(c) + r\"\\b\", side)]\n86:     if len(invs) != 1 or invs[0] not in SUPPORTED:\n87:         return None\n88:     leftover = re.sub(r\"\\b\" + re.escape(invs[0]) + r\"\\b\", \" \", side)\n89:     leftover = re.sub(r\"[()\\s]\", \"\", leftover)        # parens/space ok\n90:     return invs[0] if not leftover else None           # any coeff/const → reject\n91: \n92: \n93: def render_conditioned(native, columns) -> Optional[str]:\n94:     \"\"\"Self-rendered theorem for a class-conditioned conjecture with a simple\n95:     ``invariant REL invariant`` body, else None. The hypothesis is the conjunction\n96:     of supported class predicates.\"\"\"\n97:     try:\n98:         pretty = native.pretty()\n99:     except Exception:\n100:         return None\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":112,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:57:49Z","thinking":"**Defining the Conjecture's Scope**\n\nThe task involves a conjecture relating graph properties. The focus is establishing the meaning of `Candidate`, which seems to represent the core proposition. It appears the conjecture is being formalized for testing with Lean.\n\n\n**Investigating Potential Counterexamples**\n\nThe conjecture's \"open\" status and lack of pipeline counterexamples raise suspicion. The initial systematic searches may have been insufficient, warranting a focused search up to size 12 for graphs. The goal is to design a Python script to search graphs that may violate the inequality, specifically targeting graphs with a large radius and small Roman domination number.  The condition $radius + 1 > \\gamma_R$ implies a situation where radius is greater than or equal to the Roman domination number.\n\n\n**Analyzing Graph Radius Constraints**\n\nThe goal is to determine the radius of a connected graph covered by *k* radius-1 balls and *m* isolated vertices, derived from a Roman dominating function. Considering a vertex *c* and examining its eccentricity to find an upper bound for the radius. Observing the set S, formed by the union of ball centers and isolated vertices. Further analysis is needed to determine the shortest path distance in G between the members of S.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c \\\"\\nimport networkx as nx\\nimport time\\n\\n# How fast is roman_domination_number on 8 vertices?\\n# For n=8, brute force 3^8 = 6561.\\n# In C or vectorized numpy or bitmask it takes microseconds!\\nimport numpy as np\\n\\n# Let's test checking all graphs on 8 vertices\\n# We can generate non-isomorphic graphs using geng if we had it, or networkx / graph6\\n# Is there a graph6 file of 8-vertex graphs?\\nimport os\\nos.system('find / -name \\\\\\\"*graph8*\\\\\\\" -o -name \\\\\\\"*g8*\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null | head -10')\\n\\\"\"","Cwd":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Check for 8-vertex graph files\"","toolSummary":"\"Check graph files\""}}]}
