{"step_index":119,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:58:06Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:58:06+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:58:10+02:00\nThe search for \"roman domination number radius graph inequality bound\" returned the following summary:\nThe **Roman domination number**, denoted by $\\gamma_R(G)$, is a graph invariant that models resource allocation for network defense[1][2]. While there is extensive literature on bounds for $\\gamma_R(G)$ in terms of parameters like order ($n$), maximum degree ($\\Delta$), minimum degree ($\\delta$), and the domination number ($\\gamma(G)$), its specific relationship with the **graph radius** ($rad(G)$) is often explored in the context of broader comparative studies of graph invariants[3][4].\n\n### General Context of Bounds\n*   **Relation to Domination Number:** It is well-established that $\\gamma(G) \\le \\gamma_R(G) \\le 2\\gamma(G)$ for any graph $G$ without isolated vertices[5][6].\n*   **Degree-Based Bounds:** Lower bounds often incorporate the maximum degree $\\Delta$. For example, $\\gamma_R(G) \\ge \\frac{\\Delta+2\\delta}{\\Delta+\\delta}\\gamma(G)$ has been proved to generalize previous results[5][6].\n*   **Diameter and Girth:** Research exists that specifically investigates bounds on the Roman domination number in relation to the **diameter** and **girth** of a graph[7].\n\n### Roman Domination and Radius\nThere is no single \"standard\" inequality that links the Roman domination number exclusively to the radius for all graphs, but researchers often compare them in specific graph classes (such as trapezoid graphs or trees)[3]. \n\n*   **Comparative Studies:** Papers in graph theory often examine how different domination parameters—including Roman, total, and distance-k domination—behave as the radius or eccentricity of the graph varies[3]. \n*   **Distance-k Variants:** Because the radius is a distance-based parameter, it is frequently studied alongside **distance-k Roman domination**[8]. In these variants, the \"reach\" of a defending vertex (the distance at which it can protect other vertices) is explicitly bounded, creating a more direct link betw\n<truncated 1637 bytes>\nlg9BjycmafsOU9x_tZ2dFraGSLiqTxrVkH_ZTZeIEFHtW1_t7R3q9iP1zeL-sVRjs60dk=)\n[4] [youtube.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHNsLp7_L-jicHatkx8cR0NIvRA2MY-KPVmPD8wPWc4noetVipSx24mMFajE-0CglW1hWqYhdW1Y95gl4uJLXyYdrD4mzX7ENP3MEjmN0CTTj-Ij2SznuP2wHskFdcfJxtT)\n[5] [aimspress.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHz3RQOtPSlRfQOikgpq0YhMSNjncWT7bz_cWmZodIxkkI_ehdGkS0o9PNiM_gsYU8ax79bDpKSqMUYbDTRytDMMNVQljEhhRRG71-Kr2zeOVLa0NgojeBjDeOJRwAK6592kyzo8gG77wiRos-ALrwB1LI-GPegbIHa3NIVScc=)\n[6] [aimspress.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHz3RQOtPSlRfQOikgpq0YhMSNjncWT7bz_cWmZodIxkkI_ehdGkS0o9PNiM_gsYU8ax79bDpKSqMUYbDTRytDMMNVQljEhhRRG71-Kr2zeOVLa0NgojeBjDeOJRwAK6592kyzo8gG77wiRos-ALrwB1LI-GPegbIHa3NIVScc=)\n[7] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHFOKG3Ih33gKbdG0LdZSY-JsYI2NbQlbzmvimYcgWbDviqmgu6IPWmSSuzCpyyJLWE93KRmhvqvGq8ziAy72yodSPvALd0YzqzMYVaxZQqUBlFdox7P9MF_vt13GxBgi3f1Sw7JQ5wX95Av_zed3ARKRHC-KOTMkTjqYYoKlhbRtEAaXQ_7pOQN9htc5wdg5o2B0yu7w==)\n[8] [ejpam.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFKJ1QwJWE8rF3k7DJtRvk6VmY_BxGaX3yxkDksSG9b7dJ7RCcGUnHowsEjgC3hZYqdM9OCjgvZt0XH2bG7kOvR753SlmxqgZd8yr96yublOLQogAmlhnl44mWxYk2O0J_h5pllMTz9ew==)\n[9] [imsa.edu](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGaK2YNP21zMj_cMFoRilJckBpW421mnndzgsPRmO4TSZYamrxzDxI1z3TCqoQw0Q0_BfR3fAzJfjkhOY2t5tmsGqw_wREoRJt0ye2rl2Io9T58dv3FcAi2QWskXnLTmDpGvpHSxAp_rP_pQETM2abWElX-pFzOcYIaiR4AuRlI35G4_cKOyQ==)\n[10] [mdpi.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHxwj9zij8jA3jlB786ElS5uhDCzP__1vXV-Srrl8iDrZokwYh692kjJzmIh2TZsnJVkzzWLmBttZ5-