{"step_index":121,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:58:12Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:58:12+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:58:16+02:00\nThe search for \"\"Roman domination number\" \"diameter\" \"radius\" \"girth\"\" returned the following summary:\nIn graph theory, the **Roman domination number** ($\\gamma_R(G)$) is a variation of the classical domination number where vertices are labeled with 0, 1, or 2 such that every vertex labeled 0 is adjacent to at least one vertex labeled 2, and the total weight (sum of labels) is minimized[1][2].\n\nResearchers frequently study the relationships between the Roman domination number and other fundamental graph invariants such as **diameter**, **radius**, and **girth** to establish bounds and characterize specific graph classes[3][4][5].\n\n### Key Relationships and Bounds\n\n*   **Diameter ($\\text{diam}(G)$):** The diameter—the maximum distance between any two vertices—is often used to bound the Roman domination number[6][7]. For instance, research has shown that for connected graphs with a sufficiently large Roman domination number (e.g., $\\gamma_R(G) > 3$), there are upper bounds on the diameter[8]. Conversely, studies explore how the diameter and other parameters constrain the minimum weight of a Roman dominating function[3][4].\n*   **Girth ($\\text{gir}(G)$):** The girth—the length of the shortest cycle in a graph—is another parameter used to refine bounds on $\\gamma_R(G)$[9][7]. Specific results exist for graphs with restricted girth, such as triangle-free graphs (girth $\\geq 4$) or graphs with higher girth, where the Roman domination number is analyzed in relation to the graph's structure[10].\n*   **Radius ($\\text{rad}(G)$):** While less commonly paired directly with Roman domination than diameter, the radius (the minimum eccentricity of any vertex) is often investigated alongside diameter in extremal graph theory problems[7][11]. Bounds for domination-related parameters frequently incorporate radius to describe the \"spread\" of the graph[12][11].\n\n### Research Context\n*   **Extremal Problems:** Much of the literature focuses on findin\n<truncated 2432 bytes>\nPgV_eFQ9GNb8TvjQ==)\n[7] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE9Dy4XUR96bjLswUKETrcsOiJXNNxJefsrS6LFEUT5nZ1OTYA4Up1q35PD9gde8l4doQUD7yzGwzcXbNw2z2ULKFGITLrD3yY0mBZ-tPij7lyammqNVcDACA==)\n[8] [scribd.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGq3tyvRIm8TIHuw9MhcTWumgDZcLp5-e8gVtWA2lCsK7B9sq7ybYeqN5t8BYVAAkjg14O12vBe9WH5Oiact6DnSEgI06IlZJBiYH711g8bxj27mXAn511ZSrouGZtZMgkFdVxqXILcV0WvZ8wpTRUXmxiuX8poCY4BDA==)\n[9] [worldscientific.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGLXCE2aT-CnVCjx6kD5cyqXXXRcgcSn6JVFFrVFmCdDJcxa54IWJ2NkHFuKp2my0L5o2rIIrjL0o8M8PwUg-5DPHYE7jW71F9nB5UOdtnKT3-TP6G5CDneg9LPQSbr7GSsk1oTVua8EQ_qiMYNu1kB6ab7bq-5bHqqC6itF76IXSFNQY4deQ==)\n[10] [d-nb.info](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGSbcHWBOnoTMGtJ56_R0DWUiwmdwmt_t93p8jdTYdc9BCEezL4Y3VljLdnztk7YLU0hjLIbMh9JOo0iNPyv18pnXWU1jv63bRCLKrjcIcsol5KLwM9)\n[11] [amazonaws.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEE__mn65HHJ9oe5_rA9oV-yAjbpUWsA0v7F2_E1BtpH_XxtpRD6UyusVJzpvEcFo5Lbm5569BVkljvUDdFgZjZIHhEpFwMa5rx5xDB_MWbAKNFgu3qyuEO_IoUhYXw3gvn0dIGbqEB_0kL6kiA0F5vqbPIhRbQKjEQITjQpo1CGg==)\n[12] [dokumen.pub](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHH4w6oGKW0EyRfmk_bkfklIVad5YeapEyeWSp64jsQoN63bv35TdywawOeTqhhVKW6JpwHSLu8zLTFP3Q5dJjT0r59RwsPKRHtXIj3t32XXJB5j1Bq1_x7EWvVSZYy8mylE6nnWbX1B5iR8x9jOmzTJqcU0moXpX2378c81BJOM1cRjlK9b5yQN0dWSt70-BNHqj7d5QnZydWJ2gWteGCrKElSiJe-ren6HIVgjv3NhQ==)\n[13] [illinois.edu](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEmSZ7xymkBrZC7_5hFg-m09pPcWKzfzPB7CqVdWO5XiSkaGPZ1rjMU2VAORtHSVmHyueFXvJ2EgsiarfOFAf0KvWhOeHouviQvc1OXWdTbFb0bDmx91fazutBIDf_jn_BMUJ