{"step_index":123,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:58:18Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:58:18+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:58:22+02:00\nThe search for \"\"diam(G)\" \"roman domination\" \"\\gamma_R(G)\" bound\" returned the following summary:\nThe study of the **Roman domination number** $\\gamma_R(G)$ in relation to the **diameter** $\\text{diam}(G)$ of a graph $G$ is a well-established area in graph theory[1][2]. Research in this field typically focuses on establishing sharp lower and upper bounds for $\\gamma_R(G)$ by incorporating the graph's diameter and often its girth $g(G)$ or other parameters[1][2].\n\n### Key Context and Findings\n*   **General Bounds:** While many bounds for $\\gamma_R(G)$ are expressed in terms of the number of vertices $n$, maximum degree $\\Delta$, or domination number $\\gamma(G)$, specific studies have successfully derived bounds specifically using the diameter[1][3].\n*   **Diameter and Girth:** Papers such as *Bounds on Roman domination numbers of graphs* (Mobaraky and Sheikholeslami) explicitly address finding sharp lower and upper bounds for $\\gamma_R(G)$ in terms of both the diameter and the girth of the graph[1][2].\n*   **Relationship to $\\gamma(G)$:** The Roman domination number is closely bounded by the standard domination number $\\gamma(G)$. For example, it is known that $\\gamma_R(G) \\geq \\frac{\\Delta+2\\delta}{\\Delta+\\delta}\\gamma(G)$ and that $\\gamma(G) \\leq \\gamma_R(G) \\leq 2\\gamma(G)$ for many classes of graphs (including regular graphs)[3].\n*   **Diameter-2 Graphs:** Special attention is often given to graphs with $\\text{diam}(G) = 2$. For these graphs, researchers often characterize specific conditions under which $\\gamma_R(G)$ reaches certain bounds or compare it against planar graph constraints[4][5][6].\n\n### Notable References\nIf you are looking for the specific mathematical inequalities, the literature generally points to the work of **B.P. Mobaraky and S.M. Sheikholeslami** regarding the combined influence of diameter and girth[1][2], and various studies by **M. Chellali** and others regarding the bounds of $\\gamma_R(G)$ relative to other\n<truncated 162 bytes>\nlished in journals like *Mathematica Vesnik*), which is the primary source for the direct relationship between $\\text{diam}(G)$ and $\\gamma_R(G)$[1][2].\n\nSources:\n[1] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGERgH-ewyvqcZRz6XZ_pv2b5vzXdelvnWnUcTD3uMG2EugUic6QyqY8kjZ8cZSnvKxhINSy4Q3S1PfdIXaSl43hCWyihJcwCcV7r4l-C-8QGQG4xD_FGt-vJMUjABLviAr1jNLgvYDWOzNM1qL38HS68MLF-xTdMEpZwfJ2_XrPkH28Ca2Gh7nYelxr9vsPYlZ1wDzDA==)\n[2] [sanu.ac.rs](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGkCEz9tmUXXQwPkFpt7-xdjzn7MlLpkxOLwV3uIFjblIxOR4XkcgtpP6vH9bz8DH9ZzVGIUbzgJ85j-rKWP6HxNkQEtb_EBYYtVCUCiEQrQ36hs5035WQBQLvLxynPWKMvI-lTseW09qB6hBY=)\n[3] [aimspress.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFjUnlAMzSirlqgLKpJoZqFsbrWJAjvThcs9gYgOGOOZ_LDC3kR0KBc21lkAgE9d7P5kH-CijsPRriZZRd3yuUZ7TXS-RTZlidDd1A2z8c4KJhsZ8_9mEdUeZMKARl4sXcZ4SvThg8tw-bagdMmXLxiE8srcDuoPMyE6iaoKBM=)\n[4] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFksZsaFaVMVW8ng6YGCXcESjuR--wqVVyTzHrJr4bKS_mTAwgdAwugh-VzZkxCjhAEhqupIuyuiu2ZW9W0dXvjiA0H2k6uJq2XzDfLPuv6TXZAfeSTuJK2EnE7cJBfc2swoLGiZGoTbzPrbcaZgvCS5I___nng8S70rwAxTsTFfcwqTMsA6IlFqE2PyekvnuYKzPk=)\n[5] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHZfTU4c-dw2dZ7UVjqZkR1iUuy4L03Yhe3WfuUh44x8r2m7_hhbWLxXYRQTQrx3Y2guZgOqnJJayWIbdpZGSjwU255YZKqT10Zxp3p0f1EK-UFSstWhEQbgZoIINaMYAaL0LKzxBrfM98dOQ3fikCOcAeN9A9IbMCx2L0x-6y4QBde4y_rT-bpFm8Jk2q9Wz8zq1fQPNzotw==)\n[6] [scribd.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE3KwzhaAQ9wqr-LsFJmAdTHUoEEeZ73RUjFjfLHZmVSfLv5ddMlKOvPgBf_LsCYoq4c-5Bg2i98ouT3wjZmrQ_kgQbm8DlKx4nL_sUecupu7ArcF0EU3XheSjldz