{"step_index":131,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:58:36Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:58:36+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:58:39+02:00\nThe search for \"\"Mobaraky\" \"Sheikholeslami\" \"\\gamma_R(G) \\ge\"\" returned the following summary:\nThe research paper **\"Bounds on Roman domination numbers of graphs\"** by **B.P. Mobaraky and S.M. Sheikholeslami** (published in *Matematicki Vesnik*, 60, 2008, pp. 247–253)[1][2][3] investigates various lower and upper bounds for the Roman domination number, denoted by $\\gamma_R(G)$[4][1].\n\nWhile the specific mathematical inequality starting with $\\gamma_R(G) \\ge$ depends on the graph class or parameters (such as diameter or girth) being discussed in the paper, their work commonly establishes bounds related to these graph invariants[4][1][5].\n\nIf you are looking for a specific theorem or inequality from this paper, it is likely one of the following types of results presented by the authors:\n*   **Bounds in terms of order and other parameters:** The paper frequently relates $\\gamma_R(G)$ to the order of the graph ($n$), the diameter, or the girth ($g(G)$)[4][1][5].\n*   **Relations to the domination number:** The authors discuss the relationship between the Roman domination number $\\gamma_R(G)$ and the standard domination number $\\gamma(G)$[1].\n\nFor the exact inequality, you may wish to consult the text of the *Matematicki Vesnik* article[2][3] or their related work on Roman domination subdivision numbers[6][7].\n\nSources:\n[1] [sanu.ac.rs](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFD8rSsq6FLoprTTsNrg13usmPsVY_HPGQpMTmUaYnfgshN9tL4pC6e63KsF6zNJpyVX9Ewr-pabfNvGcoNYA6sstU7-Z18W6EZH5TZdqDvPCI9aDMFB56BAKf8cNVIKzCz_LvNXXyozkIhmBUyWM8Z)\n[2] [dagstuhl.de](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGkTPWfqA4A6dcxWfV4GoVpRFTGozE2CfTONmSTLfloXITv2xS8d6Peq-JIigexbuvYBRbQVk-Y2ylRN9q1RQBhZ3VlS4BwU972jrmp4mxx_BJr7Ac